For both detectors we started with the camera frame, blurred it a little to get rid of noise, and converted it to HSV because it makes separating colors and brightness way easier.

For the stop sign, we first look for red pixels and turn that into a mask. From that mask OpenCV finds the contours/outlines of the red objects. We filter out stuff that's too big, small, stretched out, etc. Then we simplify the contour into its main corner points and look for 8 points since a stop sign is an octagon. After that we check how close the angles are to 135 degrees, how similar the side lengths are, the aspect ratio, and some other shape stuff to make sure it's actually shaped like a stop sign. We also check brightness because a red traffic light can get picked up by the same red mask, so if the red region looks extremely bright/glowing like a light we reject it from the stop_sign detector. We made the shape requirements a little more forgiving at farther distances because the sign has fewer pixels and the corners aren't as clean.

For the traffic light, we also convert the frame to HSV, but this time we make separate masks for red, yellow, and green. We combine the color detection with brightness so we're mainly looking for bright colored regions instead of every random red/yellow/green object in the frame. OpenCV finds contours around those regions and makes candidate boxes. Inside each candidate we compare how strong the red, yellow, and green pixels are and pick whichever color has the highest power. Then we do another brightness check using stuff like the average brightness, peak brightness, and how much of the region is really bright. That's mainly there so something like a green toothbrush or yellow button doesn't automatically count as a traffic light just because the color matches. Once it passes everything, we tighten the box around the actual bulb and return the color and location.

Then main.py simply grabs each camera frame, sends the same frame through both detectors, gets their results back, and draws the boxes and results on the screen. 